<BaseLayout title="LI4 (Hegu) — Union Valley Acupressure Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate LI4 (Hegu) — the Union Valley point. Benefits include pain relief, headache relief, and immune system support.">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">LI4 (Hegu) — Union Valley</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">LI4, also known as Hegu or Union Valley, is one of the most widely used acupressure points on the body. Located on the hand, it serves as a powerful master point for the head and face.</p>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/li4-location.png" alt="LI4 acupressure point location on the hand" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" />
    <p class="mb-3">LI4 is located on the back of the hand in the web between the thumb and index finger. To find it, squeeze your thumb against your index finger — the point is at the highest point of the muscle bulge, approximately at the midpoint of the second metacarpal bone.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Pain relief:</strong> LI4 is one of the most powerful analgesic points. It is used for headaches, toothaches, facial pain, and tension headaches.</li>
      <li><strong>Immune support:</strong> Regular stimulation may help strengthen the immune system and reduce the frequency of colds and flu.</li>
      <li><strong>Sinus and nasal congestion:</strong> LI4 helps open the sinuses and relieve stuffiness associated with allergies and colds.</li>
      <li><strong>Stress and tension:</strong> It promotes relaxation and reduces stress-related muscle tension in the neck and shoulders.</li>
      <li><strong>Digestive support:</strong> LI4 can help relieve constipation and regulate bowel movements.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">How to Massage LI4</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Place the thumb of your opposite hand over the LI4 point.</li>
      <li>Apply firm, steady pressure using a small circular motion or a steady push.</li>
      <li>Maintain pressure for 30–60 seconds while breathing deeply.</li>
      <li>Repeat on the other hand. You may feel a dull ache or mild sensation — this is normal.</li>
    </ol>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">LI4 is one of the most extensively studied acupoints. A 2017 meta-analysis in <em>BMJ Open</em> found that LI4 stimulation significantly reduced pain intensity across multiple conditions.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28249892/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1</a></sup> Research in <em>Evidence-Based Complementary and Alternative Medicine</em> showed that LI4 acupressure increased pain threshold and activated brain regions associated with pain modulation.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25254057/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup></p>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2020 study in <em>Frontiers in Neuroscience</em> used fMRI to demonstrate that LI4 stimulation activates the anterior cingulate cortex and insula — key areas in the descending pain inhibitory pathway.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33192289/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3</a></sup></p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Avoid during pregnancy:</strong> LI4 stimulation is contraindicated in pregnancy, especially the third trimester, as it may induce uterine contractions.</li>
      <li>Do not apply pressure over broken skin, scars, or infections on the hand.</li>
      <li>Use gentle pressure on children, the elderly, or those with fragile bones.</li>
      <li>If you experience sharp pain, numbness, or tingling, stop immediately.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Related Points</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">LI4 works well with <a href="/points/li11-quchi-acupressure-point/">LI11 (Quchi)</a> for immunity and allergies. For headaches, combine with <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/">GB20 (Fengchi)</a> and <a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/">LV3 (Taichong)</a>.</p>
  </section></article>
